<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Mass and weight are often used interchangeably in everyday language, but they represent distinct physical concepts. Understanding the difference is crucial for accurately describing the interaction of objects with gravity. This section will clarify these two fundamental concepts.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Definition</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p><b>Mass</b> is a measure of the amount of matter in an object. It's a fundamental property that reflects an object's resistance to acceleration (inertia). Mass is a scalar quantity, meaning it has magnitude but no direction.</p>
      <p><b>Weight</b>, on the other hand, is the force exerted on an object due to gravity. It's a vector quantity, possessing both magnitude and direction (downward, towards the center of the gravitational field).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li><b>Inertia:</b> Mass is directly related to inertia. An object with more mass has more inertia and thus resists changes in its motion more strongly.</li>
        <li><b>Gravity:</b> Weight is dependent on the gravitational field. The stronger the gravitational field, the greater the weight of an object with a given mass.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Formula</div>
    <div class="formula-card">
      <div class="formula-card-title">Weight Formula</div>
      <div class="formula-card-formula">
        $$W = mg$$
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card-explanation">
        Where:
        <ul>
          <li>\(W\) = Weight (measured in Newtons, N)</li>
          <li>\(m\) = Mass (measured in kilograms, kg)</li>
          <li>\(g\) = Acceleration due to gravity (approximately 9.8 m/s² on Earth)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="formula-card-buttons">
        <button class="formula-card-button">Explain Formula</button>
        <button class="formula-card-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Characteristics</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p><b>Mass:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Is constant regardless of location.</li>
        <li>Measured in kilograms (kg) or grams (g).</li>
        <li>A measure of inertia.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><b>Weight:</b></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Varies depending on the gravitational field.</li>
        <li>Measured in Newtons (N).</li>
        <li>A force.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Applications</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Understanding mass and weight is vital in various fields:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Physics:</b> Essential for understanding Newton's laws of motion.</li>
        <li><b>Engineering:</b> Critical for structural design and calculations.</li>
        <li><b>Astronautics:</b> Necessary for calculating forces and trajectories in space.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Advantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p><b>Mass:</b> Provides a fundamental, unchanging measure of an object's matter.</p>
      <p><b>Weight:</b> Allows for direct calculation of the gravitational force acting on an object.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Disadvantages</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p><b>Mass:</b> Difficult to measure directly without specialized equipment.</p>
      <p><b>Weight:</b> Varies with location, making it less fundamental than mass.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Important Notes</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>An object's mass remains the same whether it's on Earth, the Moon, or in space. However, its weight changes depending on the gravitational pull of the celestial body it's on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Summary</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <p>Mass is the amount of matter in an object and is constant. Weight is the force of gravity acting on an object and depends on the gravitational field. While related, they are distinct and crucial concepts in physics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>What would happen to your weight if you went to the moon?</li>
        <li>If you doubled the mass of an object, what would happen to its weight?</li>
        <li>Explain the relationship between mass, inertia, and force.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
```

